 ## Background
-The volume rendering equation with extinction and inscattering is derived from the Radiative Transfer Equation (RTE), a fundamental equation in physics describing light transport in participating media [S1, unofficial]. It generalizes the Beer-Lambert law by including scattering and emission terms. The method is widely used in computer graphics for rendering volumetric effects like fog, smoke, and clouds [S1, unofficial]. The phase function concept, introduced to model directional scattering, is central to this model [S1, unofficial].
+The volume rendering equation with extinction and inscattering is described as a physically accurate model that accounts for light attenuation (absorption and out-scattering) and light gain (emission and in-scattering) by integrating over the sphere of directions, replacing the simplified single-light-source approximation [S1, unofficial]. It generalizes the Beer-Lambert law by including scattering and emission terms. The phase function concept, introduced to model directional scattering, is central to this model [S1, unofficial].
 
